    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A planned city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a> that grew to perhaps 100,000 people, among the largest in the world in the 3rd–6th centuries CE.</li><li>Its pyramids, obsidian, art and talud-tablero architecture influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a> as far as the Maya cities of Tikal and Copán.</li><li>No ruler is named in its art; around 550 its centre was burned, probably in a revolt, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> later revered its ruins.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 2003 archaeologists found a sealed tunnel about 100 metres long beneath the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-the-feathered-serpent">Feathered Serpent Pyramid</a>, from which they later recovered tens of thousands of offerings and traces of liquid mercury.</p></aside>
    </section>

