    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cuicuilco"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cuicuilco.jpg" alt="Cuicuilco" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cuicuilco">Cuicuilco</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Western_side_of_the_circular_pyramid_at_Cuicuilco._(32961693441).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Matthew T. Bradley, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Teotihuacan grew out of farming villages in the Teotihuacan Valley, a side valley in the north-east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Basin of Mexico</a>, well watered by springs and close to major sources of obsidian. In the last centuries BCE it was one of several growing centres in the basin, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuicuilco">Cuicuilco</a> in the south as its main rival. Volcanic activity helped to shift this balance: eruptions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popocatepetl">Popocatépetl</a>, probably in the first century CE, displaced communities to the east, and the eruption of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xitle">Xitle</a> buried Cuicuilco under lava, although the date of that eruption is debated and may be considerably later. Teotihuacan drew in much of the basin’s population, and during the Tzacualli phase (c. 1–150 CE) it grew with remarkable speed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyramid-of-the-sun">Pyramid of the Sun</a>, some 65 metres high, and early stages of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyramid-of-the-moon">Pyramid of the Moon</a> were raised in this period, and the city was laid out along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avenue-of-the-dead">Avenue of the Dead</a> on a grid oriented about 15.5 degrees east of north. Around 200–250 CE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-the-feathered-serpent">Feathered Serpent Pyramid</a> was built inside the vast enclosure known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciudadela-teotihuacan">Ciudadela</a>. By about 250 CE the city covered some 20 square kilometres and housed perhaps 100,000 people, making it one of the largest cities in the world. Its original name is unknown; Teotihuacan is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> name later given to it by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/otumba"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otumba.jpg" alt="Otumba" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/otumba">Otumba</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PlazaOtumbaMX.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Thelmadatter, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Most Teotihuacanos lived in more than 2,000 walled, single-storey apartment compounds, each housing perhaps 60 to 100 people, probably related families who shared courtyards, altars and crafts. Some compounds were occupied by migrants: the Tlailotlacan district on the western edge housed people from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-oaxaca">Valley of Oaxaca</a> who kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zapotec-civilization">Zapotec-style</a> tombs and pottery for centuries, and the so-called Merchants’ Barrio in the east contained pottery from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-coast-of-mexico">Gulf Coast</a> and the Maya lowlands. Farming of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, beans, squash and amaranth, aided by spring-fed irrigation in the valley, fed the population, together with maguey cultivation and hunting. Craft production was central to the economy. Workshops turned obsidian from the nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otumba">Otumba</a> sources and the green obsidian of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-de-las-navajas">Sierra de las Navajas</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachuca">Pachuca</a> into blades and points, and green Teotihuacan obsidian travelled widely across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>. Potters produced cylindrical tripod vessels, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thin-orange-ware">Thin Orange ware</a>, made in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puebla-state">Puebla</a>, was distributed through the city’s networks. Religion pervaded urban life. Murals in compounds such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tepantitla">Tepantitla</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetitla">Tetitla</a> depict a storm god related to the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlaloc">Tlaloc</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quetzalcoatl">Feathered Serpent</a> and a female deity often called the Great Goddess, and figurines and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> burners reveal household ritual. Teotihuacan used a system of glyphic signs, but whether it amounted to full writing is still debated.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/chak-tok-ichaak-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chak-tok-ichaak-i.jpg" alt="Chak Tok Ich'aak I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/chak-tok-ichaak-i">Chak Tok Ich'aak I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tikal_St26.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">HJPD, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Teotihuacan art is full of warriors armed with the atlatl, or spear-thrower, darts and rectangular shields, and of goggle-eyed figures associated with war and the storm god. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-the-feathered-serpent">Feathered Serpent Pyramid</a> gives the clearest evidence of organised state violence: when it was built around 200–250 CE, well over a hundred sacrificial victims, many of them young men with their hands bound behind their backs and some wearing necklaces of real or imitation human jaws, were buried in its foundations, and the total is estimated at around 200. Such deposits show that a military ideology was embedded in the state from early on. How far Teotihuacan projected armed force beyond central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> is debated. Within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Basin of Mexico</a> and neighbouring valleys it seems to have dominated settlement directly. At greater distances, the clearest case is the Maya city of Tikal, where inscriptions record the arrival in January 378 of a figure named Siyaj K’ak’, on the same day that the reigning king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chak-tok-ichaak-i">Chak Tok Ich’aak</a> died. A new king, Yax Nuun Ayiin, son of a lord known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spearthrower-owl">Spearthrower Owl</a>, possibly a ruler of Teotihuacan itself, was installed soon afterwards and depicted in Teotihuacan-style dress. Many scholars read this as an intervention backed by Teotihuacan, though its scale and character remain contested. Kaminaljuyu in highland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> also shows strong Teotihuacan connections.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pyramid-of-the-sun"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pyramid-of-the-sun.jpg" alt="Pyramid of the Sun" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pyramid-of-the-sun">Pyramid of the Sun</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2020-02-11_Teotihuacan_la_Avenida_de_los_Muertos_y_la_Pir%C3%A1mide_del_Sol.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Burkhard Mücke, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Teotihuacan’s political organisation is one of the great puzzles of Mesoamerican archaeology. Unlike the Maya cities, it left no inscribed monuments naming rulers, no portraits of kings and, so far, no securely identified royal tomb. In its early centuries, the scale of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyramid-of-the-sun">Pyramid of the Sun</a> and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-the-feathered-serpent">Feathered Serpent Pyramid</a>, with its mass sacrifices, suggests powerful rulers able to command enormous amounts of labour. Some scholars, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-cowgill">George Cowgill</a>, have argued that strongly personal rule gave way after about 250 CE to a more collective form of government, perhaps a council drawn from elite lineages or a system of co-rulers. The covering of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quetzalcoatl">Feathered Serpent</a> Pyramid’s sculpted facade by a plain platform, the Adosada, has been read as a sign of such a political change. What is clear is that the city was administered with great capacity. Its grid plan, standard orientation and uniform construction of apartment compounds imply central planning and regulation over centuries, and the canalisation of the San Juan River to fit the urban grid shows control over the landscape itself. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciudadela-teotihuacan">Ciudadela</a>, facing the Great Compound across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avenue-of-the-dead">Avenue of the Dead</a>, probably combined religious, political and economic functions, and the Great Compound may have held a central market. Neighbourhoods and apartment compounds probably served as intermediate units linking households to the state.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/templo-mayor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/templo-mayor.jpg" alt="Templo Mayor" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/templo-mayor">Templo Mayor</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Model_of_Tenochtitlan_Ceremonial_Center_Great_Temple_(9755406755).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gary Todd from Xinzheng, China, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Teotihuacan was the first great metropolis of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and set models that Mesoamerican peoples followed for more than a millennium. Its talud-tablero architecture, a sloping wall supporting a framed vertical panel, was copied at Kaminaljuyu, Tikal, Cholula and elsewhere, and Teotihuacan-style tripod vessels, green obsidian and images of the storm god and of goggled warriors appear across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>. In Maya cities, links to Teotihuacan became a source of prestige: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinich-yax-kuk-mo">K’inich Yax K’uk’ Mo’</a>, who founded the royal dynasty of Copán in 426, was later portrayed with Teotihuacan-style goggles. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quetzalcoatl">Feathered Serpent</a>, prominent at Teotihuacan, became central to later religion as Quetzalcoatl, and the storm god survived as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlaloc">Tlaloc</a>. After its fall the ruined city kept an enormous hold on memory. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> gave it its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> name, believed that the gods had gathered there to create the present sun, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifth-sun">Fifth Sun</a>, visited the site, and buried Teotihuacan masks and other objects in offerings at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/templo-mayor">Templo Mayor</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>. Modern excavation began in earnest with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopoldo-batres">Leopoldo Batres</a>, whose reconstruction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyramid-of-the-sun">Pyramid of the Sun</a> was completed in 1910, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-gamio">Manuel Gamio</a>’s regional study followed in the 1910s. The site was inscribed on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage List</a> in 1987 and is now one of the most visited archaeological sites in the Americas.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan.jpg" alt="Tenochtitlan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vista_completa_de_Tenochtitl%C3%A1n.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">thomas kole, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Teotihuacan’s decline began before its final catastrophe. In its last phases its influence abroad weakened, links with the Maya region faded during the late fifth and sixth centuries, and the population may already have been falling. Then, at a date usually placed around 550 CE, though some scholars argue for a somewhat later date, the civic and religious centre was deliberately set on fire. Burning swept the temples and elite buildings along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avenue-of-the-dead">Avenue of the Dead</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciudadela-teotihuacan">Ciudadela</a>, sculptures were smashed, and some bodies were left unburied. Because the destruction targeted the monuments and residences of the elite rather than ordinary apartment compounds, many archaeologists, following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-millon">René Millon</a>, interpret it as an internal uprising against the ruling class rather than a foreign conquest, although external pressure, drought and economic strain may also have played a part. The city did not vanish at once. Its population fell sharply, but a reduced settlement, marked by new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coyotlatelco">Coyotlatelco</a> pottery, continued among the ruins for centuries. Power in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> fragmented among Epiclassic centres such as Xochicalco, Cacaxtla and Cholula, and later passed to Tula. No later city in the region matched Teotihuacan’s urban scale until the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, some nine centuries after its fall.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Teotihuacan was the largest and most influential city of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, a planned metropolis of perhaps 100,000 people whose pyramids, obsidian trade, art and religion shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a> to the Maya lowlands, and whose ruins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> later revered as the birthplace of the present sun.</em></p>

